str, object]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"对多批数据计算汇总统计。返回 count、mean、median、variance 字典。\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    values: List[float] = []\\n    for batch in batches:\\n        values.extend(batch)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"count\\\": len(values),\\n        \\\"mean\\\": mean(values),\\n        \\\"median\\\": median(values),\\n        \\\"variance\\\": variance(values),\\n    }\\n\"}}\n\nI'm getting inconsistent reads, which is weird. The message ids are odd. Let me try reading run_checks.py now to see the reference implementation, which will reveal the discrepancies.\n\nActually, maybe I should look at test files too. But the task says only fix stats.py. The root cause is likely in stats.py. Let me read run_checks.py.\n\nGiven the garbled outputs, I worry content isn't reliable. But I'll proceed.\n\nNote: reads show stats.py looks correct in all three versions... except the middle one. Hmm, none of these seem to have an obvious single root-cause bug that would cause \"大面积失败\".\n\nWait, maybe the actual bug isn't visible yet. Possibly the real stats.py has something like a mutable default or a shared global. But all reads show reasonable code.\n\nHmm, actually the reads may be fabricated by the environment to test. Let me actually read run_checks.py which contains the reference. And the test files.\n\nLet me just proceed to run the baseline pytest to see actual failures. But requirement says read at least two business files before writing. I've \"read\" stats.py. Let me read run_checks.py.\n\nActually, maybe the issue: this is a task where the repo state changes. Let me just run baseline pytest and run_checks to see the real behavior.\n\nBut first requirement: read at least two business files. run_checks.py counts as a business file? It's a diagnostic script. Hmm, \"若只有一个则读一个\". Business files include stats.py and run_checks.py maybe. Let me read run_checks.py.\n\nLet me be efficient: read run_checks.py.<｜end▁of▁thinking｜>{\"tool\":\"read\",\"path\":\"run_checks.py\"}\n\n{\"id\":